Part B: Lab (75-90 min)

Step 1: Find the container’s process on the host
------------------------------------------------------
bash
docker run -d --name sleeper alpine sleep 3600
PID=$(docker inspect -f '{{.State.Pid}}' sleeper)
echo $PID
ps -fp $PID
----------------------------------------------------------
That sleep 3600 is an ordinary process, visible in the host’s process table.

Step 2: Compare views
---------------------------------------------------------------
bash
docker exec sleeper ps          # inside: sleep is PID 1
ps -fp $PID                      # outside: it has a large PID
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Same process, two PIDs. That is the PID namespace.


Step 3: Look at its namespaces
--------------------------------------------------------------------
bash
sudo ls -l /proc/$PID/ns
sudo ls -l /proc/$$/ns           # your own shell, for comparison
sudo lsns -p $PID
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Compare the inode numbers. The pid, net, mnt, uts, and ipc entries differ between the container and your shell. That is the isolation.


Step 4: Check the container’s network namespace
------------------------------------------------------------------------
bash
sudo nsenter -t $PID -n ip addr
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
nsenter runs a command inside the container’s namespace from the host. You will see eth0 with a 172.17.x.x address, and the host’s interfaces are not visible.

Step 5: Create your own “container” by hand
------------------------------------------------------
bash
sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc bash

Inside the new shell:

bash
ps aux        # only 2-3 processes; bash is PID 1
echo $$       # prints 1
exit

Now try the UTS namespace:

bash
sudo unshare --uts bash
hostname demo-container
hostname
exit
hostname      # your real hostname is unchanged
---------------------------------------------------------------------
You just did, in two commands, what Docker does automatically.

Step 6: cgroups: set limits
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bash
docker run -d --name limited -m 100m --cpus 0.5 alpine sleep 3600
ID=$(docker inspect -f '{{.Id}}' limited)
cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-$ID.scope/memory.max
cat /sys/fs/cgroup/system.slice/docker-$ID.scope/cpu.max

Expect 104857600 (100 MB) and 50000 100000 (half a CPU). If the path doesn’t exist, run cat /proc/$(docker inspect -f '{{.State.Pid}}' limited)/cgroup to find your cgroup path. Older systems use cgroup v1 with different paths.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 7: Trigger an OOM kill on purpose
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
bash
docker run --rm -m 50m --memory-swap 50m python:3.12-alpine \
  python -c "x = 'a' * (200*1024*1024); print('allocated')"
echo "exit code: $?"

Exit code 137 (128 + SIGKILL 9) means the kernel killed it. Check the kernel log: sudo dmesg | tail -5. You will see this again in Week 5 as OOMKilled in Kubernetes.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 8: See the layered filesystem
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
bash
docker inspect -f '{{json .GraphDriver.Data}}' sleeper | python3 -m json.tool

Look for LowerDir, UpperDir, and MergedDir. Then:

bash
docker run -d --name writer alpine sh -c "echo hello > /data.txt; sleep 3600"
docker diff writer       # shows A /data.txt, the file in the writable layer
docker rm -f writer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The file is gone with the container.

Step 9: Clean up

bash
docker rm -f $(docker ps -aq)
